# /etc/dnscheck/dnscheck.env   —   0600 root:root
#
# Copy to /etc/dnscheck/dnscheck.env and fill in every value. There are no
# built-in defaults for NS, ZONES or HC_URL: a fallback for those is how a
# misconfigured run ends up looking like a passing one.
#
# HC_URL IS A CREDENTIAL. Anyone holding it can forge a check-in and keep the
# dead-man switch quiet while your DNS is down. Never in a repo, never in the
# script, never in argv.
#
# Format is systemd's EnvironmentFile: KEY=value, one per line, the rest of the
# line is the value. Quotes are optional and stripped if present — write it
# either way. check-dns.sh parses this file the same way systemd does, so the
# timer and a run by hand always read it identically.

# The authoritative nameservers, as label=IP pairs. The label is yours and
# appears in every alert line, so make it something you will recognise at 3am.
#
# IPs, not hostnames, deliberately: resolving a nameserver's name in order to
# find out whether DNS is working is circular, and a broken zone would take the
# check down with it — going quiet at exactly the moment it had something to
# say. The trade-off is that this pins the addresses, so update this line if a
# nameserver is ever renumbered, or the check reports a server down that is
# merely somewhere else.
#
# The addresses below are RFC 5737 documentation ranges. Replace all three.
NS=ns1=192.0.2.1 ns2=198.51.100.1 ns3=203.0.113.1

# Zones to check, space separated, split by whether they are signed. Both
# lists are optional individually; you need at least one of them.
#
# Every zone in either list gets the checks that apply to any zone: all
# nameservers reachable, all authoritative, and all agreeing on the SOA serial.
#
# SIGNED_ZONES additionally get the DNSSEC checks — RRSIG present over SOA and
# DNSKEY, neither expiring within WARN_DAYS, and the chain validating from
# outside.
#
# Which list a zone belongs in is DECLARED, not detected. Auto-detecting
# "no DNSKEY, so it must be unsigned" would silently reclassify a zone whose
# signing had STOPPED as working exactly as intended — the precise failure this
# tool exists to catch. Being signed is a fact about your intent, so you state
# it, and moving a zone between lists is the deliberate act of changing that
# intent.
#
# Get it wrong in either direction and the check tells you: an unsigned zone in
# SIGNED_ZONES alarms about RRSIGs that were never meant to exist, and a signed
# zone in UNSIGNED_ZONES is reported the first time a validator returns AD for
# it.
#
# Each zone adds ~6 authoritative queries plus 2 validator queries per run
# (~2 fewer for an unsigned zone, which has no DNSKEY to fetch).
SIGNED_ZONES=example.com example.net
UNSIGNED_ZONES=example.org

# Recursive resolvers used to confirm the chain of trust from outside. Two, so
# that one having a bad afternoon is not mistaken for your DNSSEC being broken.
#
# Do NOT put this host's own resolver here if it is a slave for these zones —
# the check would then be partly asking itself whether it is working.
VALIDATORS=1.1.1.1 8.8.8.8

# Alarm when a signature has fewer than this many days left.
#
# This must sit BELOW the floor of your resigning cycle, not below the
# signature's validity. Those are very different numbers. BIND's default
# dnssec-policy issues RRSIGs valid for 14 days and refreshes them 5 days
# before expiry, so remaining life sawtooths between 14 days and 5 — it is
# SUPPOSED to reach 5 and then jump back up, roughly every 9 days.
#
# So a threshold of 7 fires every cycle, for the two days the zone spends
# between 7 and 5 days remaining: about a fifth of the time, always, forever,
# on a perfectly healthy zone. That is how an alert becomes something you
# learn to ignore.
#
# 3 leaves two days of slack under the 5-day refresh point — enough for the
# resign, for the transfer to the secondaries, and for the jitter BIND applies
# to expiry times so that signatures do not all come due at once. Reaching 3
# means resigning is genuinely two days overdue, and you still have three days
# to fix it before validating resolvers start failing.
#
# If your policy is not the default, the rule is the same: set this a couple of
# days below whatever your refresh threshold is. Check it against a live zone
# rather than assuming — the summary line prints the remaining days every run.
#
# Whole days, digits only, and nothing after the number — no unit, no decimal
# point, no trailing comment. The rest of the line is the value here, so
# `WARN_DAYS=3 days` is the string "3 days", and the check refuses to start
# rather than run on with expiry monitoring quietly switched off.
WARN_DAYS=3

# The healthchecks.io ping URL for THIS check — its own uuid. Sharing one with
# another check would let that check's healthy pings keep this one looking
# alive, which is the exact failure a dead-man switch exists to catch.
HC_URL=https://hc-ping.com/PUT-THE-DNSCHECK-UUID-HERE
